<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="Aldo Bini and the black jersey at the 1948 Giro d’Italia: a former star completed the race with a broken hand."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/stories/aldo-bini/aldo-bini-archive.webp"/><title>The star who fell from the top · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com"/><link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin/><link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=DM+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=Libre+Caslon+Display&display=swap" rel="stylesheet"/><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head>
<body><EnglishHeader current="stories" danishPath="/historier/aldo-bini-maglia-nera-1948/"/><main><article class="bini-page">
  <header class="bini-hero"><p class="section-label">Giro d’Italia · 1948</p><h1>The star who fell<br/>from the top — and<br/>found <em>gold at the bottom</em></h1><p>For Aldo Bini, the greatest achievement was not winning, but refusing to give up.</p></header>
  <figure class="bini-portrait"><img src={`${base}/images/stories/aldo-bini/aldo-bini-archive.webp`} alt="Historical photograph identified as Aldo Bini beside his racing bicycle"/><figcaption>Archive photograph in the collection, identified as Aldo Bini. The precise occasion and photographer are not documented.</figcaption></figure>
  <section class="bini-opening"><p class="section-label">From pink to black</p><p>Imagine having been one of Italy’s most feared sprinters, having worn the pink leader’s jersey and beaten Gino Bartali — only to discover years later that your most heroic Giro would be ridden at the very bottom of the standings.</p></section>
  <section class="bini-before"><div><p class="section-label">One of the best</p><h2>Before the fall, he was a star</h2></div><div><p>In the late 1930s, Aldo Bini was among Italy’s strongest riders. He wore the pink leader’s jersey for five days in 1936, won five Giro stages during his career and took the Giro di Lombardia in both 1937 and 1942.</p><p>His 1937 Lombardia victory came ahead of Gino Bartali. Bini was known as a sprinter, but he could also win when the road became hard.</p></div></section>
  <figure class="maglia-scene"><img src={`${base}/images/stories/aldo-bini/maglia-nera-illustration.webp`} alt="Illustration of a cyclist resting beside a road during the Giro d’Italia" loading="lazy"/><figcaption>A contemporary illustration of life around the Maglia Nera — not a historical photograph from the Giro.</figcaption></figure>
  <section class="black-jersey"><p class="section-label">Maglia Nera</p><blockquote>The black jersey turned last place into a sought-after position.</blockquote><div><p>From 1946 to 1951, the last-placed rider in the Giro was associated with the distinctive Maglia Nera. The position brought attention, meals and cash prizes.</p><p>The contest for last place could become comic tactics. Riders tried to lose time without missing the time limit — sometimes by hiding along the route or deliberately causing delays.</p></div></section>
  <section class="bini-crash"><aside><strong>1948</strong><span>The 31st Giro d’Italia</span></aside><div><p class="section-label">This time it was serious</p><h2>A broken hand and a promise to continue</h2><p>Early in the race, Bini crashed heavily and broke his right hand. He continued anyway. In the mountains the pain became so severe that he had to dismount and push his bicycle up the climbs.</p><p>For Bini, the black jersey was therefore not merely a tactical trick. It became visible proof that he had completed an entire Giro d’Italia despite an injury that would normally have ended his race.</p></div></section>
  <section class="bini-facts">{facts.map(([number,label]) => <div><strong>{number}</strong><span>{label}</span></div>)}</section>
  <section class="giro-1948"><div><p class="section-label">Milan · 6 June</p><h2>Last across the line</h2></div><div><p>After 19 stages and 4,164 kilometres, 41 of the 77 starters made it back to Milan. Fiorenzo Magni won the Giro in 124 hours, 51 minutes and 52 seconds — just 11 seconds ahead of Ezio Cecchi.</p><p>Bini finished 41st in 128 hours, 59 minutes and 43 seconds. He was 4 hours, 7 minutes and 51 seconds behind Magni.</p><p>He received the black jersey, the prizes and the respect of the crowd. The star of the 1930s had found another kind of honour: not in victory, but in stubbornness.</p></div></section>
  <section class="bini-closing"><blockquote>You can finish last<br/>and still be <em>a hero.</em></blockquote></section>
  <section class="period-sources"><p class="section-label">Sources and image notes</p><div><p>Race data has been checked against the Giro d’Italia historical archive. The Bini story and his results have been compared with cycling biographies and results archives.</p><ul><li><a href="https://www.gazzetta.it/Speciali/Giroditalia/archivio-storico/it/edizioni/edizione_33.shtml">Giro d’Italia: 1948 historical archive</a></li><li><a href="https://archivio.giroditalia.it/eng/editions/1948-1957/">Giro d’Italia: 1948–1957</a></li><li><a href="https://www.museociclismo.it/content/articoli/2546-Aldo-Bini---Il-virtuoso-e-leggendario-monello/index.html">Museo del Ciclismo: Aldo Bini</a></li></ul></div></section>
  <nav class="era-bottom-nav" aria-label="Further navigation"><a href={`${base}/en/stories/`}>← All stories</a><a href={`${base}/historier/leroica-gamle-cykler-nye-veje/`}>Next story (in Danish) →</a></nav>
</article></main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
